1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,120
為什麼要做到大語言?

2
00:00:05,120 --> 00:00:08,880
 Sometimes people think of a large language model as a knowledge store,

3
00:00:08,880 --> 00:00:13,200
 as if it learns to memorize a lot of information, maybe off the internet,

4
00:00:13,200 --> 00:00:16,560
 so when you ask the question, it can answer the question.

5
00:00:16,560 --> 00:00:20,320
 But I think an even more useful way to think of a large language model

6
00:00:20,320 --> 00:00:22,880
 is sometimes as a reasoning engine,

7
00:00:22,880 --> 00:00:27,200
 in which you can give it chunks of text or other sources of information.

8
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:31,520
 And then the large language model, LLM, will maybe use this background knowledge,

9
00:00:31,520 --> 00:00:35,360
 this learn off the internet, but to use the new information you give it

10
00:00:35,360 --> 00:00:41,040
 to help you answer questions or reason through content or decide even what to do next.

11
00:00:41,040 --> 00:00:44,640
 And that's what LangChain's Agents framework helps you to do.

12
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:48,240
 Agents are probably my favorite part of LangChain.

13
00:00:48,240 --> 00:00:50,240
 I think they're also one of the most powerful parts,

14
00:00:50,240 --> 00:00:52,160
 but they're also one of the newer parts.

15
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:55,920
 So we're seeing a lot of stuff emerge here that's really new to everyone in the field.

16
00:00:55,920 --> 00:01:00,400
 And so this should be a very exciting lesson as we dive into what agents are,

17
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:02,560
 how to create and how to use agents,

18
00:01:02,560 --> 00:01:04,560
 how to equip them with different types of tools,

19
00:01:04,560 --> 00:01:07,280
 like search engines that come built into LangChain,

20
00:01:07,280 --> 00:01:09,200
 and then also how to create your own tools,

21
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:13,440
 so that you can let agents interact with any data stores,

22
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:16,880
 any APIs, any functions that you might want them to.

23
00:01:16,880 --> 00:01:19,360
 So this is exciting cutting edge stuff,

24
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:23,120
 but already with emerging important use cases.

25
00:01:23,120 --> 00:01:24,640
 So with that, let's dive in.

26
00:01:24,640 --> 00:01:28,720
 First, we're going to set the environment variables

27
00:01:28,720 --> 00:01:31,600
 and import a bunch of stuff that we will use later on.

28
00:01:31,600 --> 00:01:35,600
 Next, we're going to initialize a language model.

29
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:37,360
 We're going to use ChatOpenAI.

30
00:01:37,360 --> 00:01:40,080
 And importantly, we're going to set the temperature equal to zero.

31
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:43,760
 This is important because we're going to be using the language model

32
00:01:43,760 --> 00:01:50,240
 as the reasoning engine of an agent where it's connecting to other sources of data and computation.

33
00:01:50,240 --> 00:01:56,160
 And so we want this reasoning engine to be as good and as precise as possible.

34
00:01:56,160 --> 00:02:00,160
 And so we're going to set it to zero to get rid of any randomness that might arise.

35
00:02:02,240 --> 00:02:04,000
 Next, we're going to load some tools.

36
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:10,000
 The two tools that we're going to load are the LLM Math tool and the Wikipedia tool.

37
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:13,840
 The LLM Math tool is actually a chain itself,

38
00:02:13,840 --> 00:02:19,440
 which uses a language model in conjunction with a calculator to do math problems.

39
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:23,920
 The Wikipedia tool is an API that connects to Wikipedia,

40
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:28,800
 allowing you to run search queries against Wikipedia and get back results.

41
00:02:28,800 --> 00:02:33,680
 Next, we're going to initialize an agent.

42
00:02:33,680 --> 00:02:38,640
 We're going to initialize the agent with the tools, the language model, and then an agent type.

43
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,720
 Here, we're going to use ChatZeroShotReactDescription.

44
00:02:42,720 --> 00:02:46,720
 The important things to note here are, first, chat.

45
00:02:46,720 --> 00:02:50,080
 This is an agent that has been optimized to work with chat models.

46
00:02:50,640 --> 00:02:52,560
 And second, react.

47
00:02:52,560 --> 00:02:58,560
 This is a prompting technique designed to get the best reasoning performance out of language models.

48
00:02:58,560 --> 00:03:02,800
 We're also going to pass in handleParsingErrors = true.

49
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:08,640
 This is useful when the language model might output something that is not able to be parsed

50
00:03:08,640 --> 00:03:13,600
 into an action and action input, which is the desired output.

51
00:03:13,600 --> 00:03:18,480
 When this happens, we'll actually pass the misformatted text back to the language model

52
00:03:18,480 --> 00:03:19,920
 and ask it to correct itself.

53
00:03:19,920 --> 00:03:23,360
 And finally, we're going to pass in verbose = true.

54
00:03:23,360 --> 00:03:28,880
 This is going to print out a bunch of steps that makes it really clear to us in the Jupyter Notebook

55
00:03:28,880 --> 00:03:29,680
 what's going on.

56
00:03:29,680 --> 00:03:34,800
 We'll also set debug = true at the global level later on in the notebook,

57
00:03:34,800 --> 00:03:37,440
 so we can see in more detail what exactly is happening.

58
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:40,800
 First, we're going to ask the agent a math question.

59
00:03:40,800 --> 00:03:43,200
 What is 25% of 300?

60
00:03:43,200 --> 00:03:47,840
 This is a pretty simple question, but it will be good to understand what exactly is going on.

61
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:52,160
 So we can see here that when it enters the agent executor chain,

62
00:03:52,160 --> 00:03:55,200
 that it first thinks about what it needs to do.

63
00:03:55,840 --> 00:03:56,720
 So it has a thought.

64
00:03:56,720 --> 00:03:59,360
 It then has an action.

65
00:03:59,360 --> 00:04:05,600
 And this action is actually a JSON blob corresponding to two things: an action and an action input.

66
00:04:05,600 --> 00:04:10,320
 The action corresponds to the tool to use, so here it says calculator.

67
00:04:10,320 --> 00:04:13,280
 The action input is the input to that tool.

68
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:17,520
 And here it's a string of 300 times 0.25.

69
00:04:17,520 --> 00:04:23,040
 Next, we can see that there's observation with answer in a separate color.

70
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:31,120
 This observation, answer = 75.0, is actually coming from the calculator tool itself.

71
00:04:31,120 --> 00:04:36,400
 Next, we go back to the language model when the text turns to green.

72
00:04:36,400 --> 00:04:38,880
 We have the answer to the question.

73
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:41,440
 Final answer, 75.0.

74
00:04:42,080 --> 00:04:43,360
 And that's the output that we get.

75
00:04:43,360 --> 00:04:50,480
 This is a good time to pause and try out different math problems of your own.

76
00:04:50,480 --> 00:04:56,240
 Next, we're going to go through an example using the Wikipedia API.

77
00:04:56,240 --> 00:04:59,680
 Here, we're going to ask it a question about Tom Mitchell.

78
00:04:59,680 --> 00:05:02,640
 And we can look at the intermediate steps to see what it does.

79
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:07,680
 We can see once again that it thinks and it correctly realizes that it should use Wikipedia.

80
00:05:10,240 --> 00:05:14,720
 It says action equal to Wikipedia and action input equal to Tom M. Mitchell.

81
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:26,480
 The observation that comes back in yellow this time and we use different colors to denote different tools is the Wikipedia summary result for the Tom M. Mitchell page.

82
00:05:28,080 --> 00:05:33,360
 The observation that comes back from Wikipedia is actually two results, two pages, as there's two different Tom M. Mitchells.

83
00:05:33,360 --> 00:05:41,360
 We can see the first one covers the computer scientist and the second one, it looks like it's an Australian footballer.

84
00:05:41,360 --> 00:05:52,480
 We can see that the information needed to answer this question, namely the name of the book that he wrote, Machine Learning, is present in the summary of the first Tom M. Mitchell.

85
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:59,360
 We can see next that the agent tries to look up more information about this book.

86
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:02,080
 So it looks up machine learning book in Wikipedia.

87
00:06:02,080 --> 00:06:08,800
 This isn't strictly necessary and it's an interesting example to show how agents aren't perfectly reliable yet.

88
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:17,440
 We can see that after this lookup, the agent recognizes that it has all the information it needs to answer and responds with the correct answer, machine learning.

89
00:06:19,280 --> 00:06:21,840
 The next example we're going to go through is a really cool one.

90
00:06:21,840 --> 00:06:27,920
 If you've seen things like Copilot or even ChatGPT with the code interpreter plugin enabled,

91
00:06:27,920 --> 00:06:34,240
 one of the things they're doing is they're using the language model to write code and then executing that code.

92
00:06:34,240 --> 00:06:36,000
 And we can do the same exact thing here.

93
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:42,480
 So we're going to create a Python agent and we're going to use the same LLM as before.

94
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:46,560
 And we're going to give it a tool, the Python REPL tool.

95
00:06:46,560 --> 00:06:49,120
 A REPL is basically a way to interact with code.

96
00:06:49,120 --> 00:06:51,120
 You can think of it as a Jupyter notebook.

97
00:06:51,120 --> 00:06:55,360
 So the agent can execute code with this REPL.

98
00:06:55,360 --> 00:06:58,560
 It will then run and then we'll get back some results.

99
00:06:58,560 --> 00:07:04,160
 And those results will be passed back into the agent so it can decide what to do next.

100
00:07:04,160 --> 00:07:10,160
 The problem that we're going to have this agent solve is we're going to give it a list of names

101
00:07:10,160 --> 00:07:11,600
 and then ask it to sort them.

102
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:21,040
 So you can see here we have a list of names, Harrison Chase, LangChain, LLM, Jeff Fusion, Transformer, GenAI.

103
00:07:21,040 --> 00:07:27,360
 And we're going to ask the agent to first sort these names by last name and then first name

104
00:07:27,360 --> 00:07:28,960
 and then print the output.

105
00:07:28,960 --> 00:07:33,760
 Importantly, we're asking it to print the output so that it can actually see what the result is.

106
00:07:35,040 --> 00:07:40,240
 These printed statements are what's going to be fed back into the language model later on so it can

107
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:43,120
 reason about the output of the code that it just ran.

108
00:07:44,960 --> 00:07:45,920
 Let's give this a try.

109
00:07:45,920 --> 00:07:54,400
 We can see that when we go into the agent executor chain, it first realizes that it can use the

110
00:07:54,400 --> 00:07:56,320
 sorted function to list the customers.

111
00:07:56,320 --> 00:08:04,720
 It's using a different agent type under the hood, which is why you can see that the action and action

112
00:08:04,720 --> 00:08:07,280
 input is actually formatted slightly differently.

113
00:08:08,240 --> 00:08:14,240
 Here, the action that it takes is to use the Python REPL and then the action input that you can see is

114
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:18,080
 code where it first writes out customers equals this list.

115
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:21,280
 It then sorts the customers and then it goes through this list and print it.

116
00:08:21,280 --> 00:08:26,880
 You can see the agent thinks about what to do and realizes that it needs to write some code.

117
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:33,280
 The format that it's using of action and action input is actually slightly different than before.

118
00:08:33,280 --> 00:08:35,920
 It's using a different agent type under the hood.

119
00:08:35,920 --> 00:08:41,760
 For the action, it's going to use the Python REPL and for the action input, it's going to have a bunch

120
00:08:41,760 --> 00:08:46,640
 of code. If we look at what this code is doing, it's first creating a variable to list out these

121
00:08:46,640 --> 00:08:52,560
 customer names. It's then sorting that and creating a new variable and it's then iterating through that

122
00:08:52,560 --> 00:08:58,800
 new variable and printing out each line, just like we asked it to. We can see that we get the observation

123
00:08:58,800 --> 00:09:04,880
 back and this is a list of names and then the agent realizes that it's done and it returns these names.

124
00:09:04,880 --> 00:09:11,120
 We can see from the stuff that's printed out the high level of what's going on,

125
00:09:11,120 --> 00:09:16,240
 but let's dig a little bit deeper and run this with langchain debug set to true.

126
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:24,560
 As this prints out all the levels of all the different chains that are going on, let's go

127
00:09:24,560 --> 00:09:29,840
 through them and see what exactly is happening. First, we start with the agent executor. This is

128
00:09:29,840 --> 00:09:34,880
 the top level agent runner and we can see that we have here our input. Sort these customers by last

129
00:09:34,880 --> 00:09:41,920
 name and then first name and print the output. From here, we call an LLM chain. This is the LLM chain

130
00:09:41,920 --> 00:09:49,520
 that the agent is using. The LLM chain, remember, is a combination of prompt and an LLM. At this point,

131
00:09:49,520 --> 00:09:54,800
 it's only got the input, an agent scratch pad, we'll get back to that later, and then some stop

132
00:09:54,800 --> 00:10:02,320
 sequences to tell the language model when to stop doing its generations. At the next level, we see the

133
00:10:02,320 --> 00:10:08,560
 exact call to the language model. So we can see the fully formatted prompt, which includes instructions

134
00:10:08,560 --> 00:10:16,720
 about what tools it has access to, as well as how to format its output. From there, we can then see the

135
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:23,440
 exact output of the language model. So we can see the text key where it has the thought and the action

136
00:10:23,440 --> 00:10:31,360
 and the action input all in one string. It then wraps up the LLM chain as it exits through there.

137
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:37,360
 And the next thing that it calls is a tool. And here we can see the exact input to the tool. We can

138
00:10:37,360 --> 00:10:41,600
 also see the name of the tool, Python REPL, and then we can see the input, which is this code.

139
00:10:41,600 --> 00:10:47,200
 We can then see the output of this tool, which is this printed out string. And again,

140
00:10:47,200 --> 00:10:52,000
 this happens because we specifically ask the Python REPL to print out what is going on.

141
00:10:54,000 --> 00:10:59,520
 We can then see the next input to the LLM chain, which again, the LLM chain here is the agent.

142
00:10:59,520 --> 00:11:06,080
 So here, if you look at the variables, there's the input. This is unchanged. This is the high

143
00:11:06,080 --> 00:11:10,320
 level objective that we're asking. But now there's some new values for agent scratchpad.

144
00:11:10,320 --> 00:11:19,280
 You can see here that this is actually a combination of the previous generation plus the tool output.

145
00:11:19,280 --> 00:11:23,920
 And so we're passing this back in so that the LLM can understand what happened previously

146
00:11:23,920 --> 00:11:30,160
 and use that to reason about what to do next. The next few print statements are covering what

147
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:36,000
 happens as the LLM realizes that it is basically finished with its job. So we can see here the

148
00:11:36,000 --> 00:11:43,760
 fully formatted prompt to the LLM, the response, where it realizes that it is done, and it says

149
00:11:43,760 --> 00:11:50,240
 final answer, which here is the sequence that the agent uses to recognize that it's done with its job.

150
00:11:50,240 --> 00:11:53,760
 We can then see it exiting the LLM chain and then exiting the agent executor.

151
00:11:53,760 --> 00:11:59,600
 This should hopefully give you a pretty good idea of what's going on under the hood inside these agents.

152
00:11:59,600 --> 00:12:04,000
 This should hopefully give you a pretty good idea of what's going on under the hood and is

153
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:10,240
 hopefully instructive as you pause and input your own objectives for this coding agent to try to

154
00:12:10,240 --> 00:12:15,760
 accomplish. This debug mode can also be used to highlight what's going wrong. As shown above in

155
00:12:15,760 --> 00:12:20,880
 the Wikipedia example, sometimes agents act a little funny. And so having all this information is really

156
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:27,840
 helpful for understanding what's going on. So far, we've used tools that come defined in LinkedIn already.

157
00:12:27,840 --> 00:12:34,080
 But a big power of agents is that you can connect it to your own sources of information, your own APIs,

158
00:12:34,080 --> 00:12:38,560
 your own data. So here we're going to go over how you can create a custom tool so that you can connect

159
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:45,280
 it to whatever you want. Let's make a tool that's going to tell us what the current date is. First,

160
00:12:45,280 --> 00:12:50,720
 we're going to import this tool decorator. This can be applied to any function and it turns it into a

161
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:58,000
 tool that LinkedIn can use. Next, we're going to write a function called time, which takes in any text

162
00:12:58,000 --> 00:13:02,720
 string. We're not really going to use that. And it's going to return today's date by calling date

163
00:13:02,720 --> 00:13:09,840
 time. In addition to the name of the function, we're also going to write a really detailed doc

164
00:13:09,840 --> 00:13:15,600
 string. That's because this is what the agent will use to know when it should call this tool and how it

165
00:13:15,600 --> 00:13:22,080
 should call this tool. For example, here we say that the input should always be an empty string.

166
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:27,520
 That's because we don't use it. If we have more stringent requirements on what the input should be,

167
00:13:27,520 --> 00:13:31,440
 for example, if we have a function that should always take in a search query

168
00:13:31,440 --> 00:13:34,640
 or a SQL statement, you'll want to make sure to mention that here.

169
00:13:34,640 --> 00:13:41,120
 We're now going to create another agent. This time we're adding the time tool

170
00:13:41,120 --> 00:13:42,720
 to the list of existing tools.

171
00:13:42,720 --> 00:13:49,040
 And finally, let's call the agent and ask it what the date today is.

172
00:13:52,080 --> 00:13:57,520
 It recognizes that it needs to use the time tool, which it specifies here.

173
00:13:57,520 --> 00:14:02,080
 It has the action input as an empty string. This is great. This is what we told it to do.

174
00:14:02,080 --> 00:14:07,360
 And then it returns with an observation. And then finally, the language model takes that observation

175
00:14:07,360 --> 00:14:14,320
 and responds to the user. Today's date is 2023-05-21. You should pause the video here

176
00:14:14,320 --> 00:14:21,040
 and try putting in different inputs. This wraps up the lesson on agents. This is one of the newer and more

177
00:14:21,040 --> 00:14:27,440
 exciting and more experimental pieces of Langchain. So I hope you enjoy using it. Hopefully it showed

178
00:14:27,440 --> 00:14:32,000
 you how you can use a language model as a reasoning engine to take different actions

179
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:34,480
 and connect to other functions and data sources.

